# The half of `drain` that is not delivery, on a tick that suits it.
#
# ── what moved here and why ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `drain` fires every minute, and until this file it also ran the check-in
# before a WhatsApp window shuts, the plan notices, the daily digest and the
# WhatsApp cost alert — each of which begins by asking the database whether it
# has anything to do. Five questions a minute, 7,200 a day, to find work that
# by its own nature appears at most once a day per subscriber.
#
# None of them needs a minute. The digest is daily and keyed on (user_id, day).
# A plan notice is once per period. The check-in guards a window twenty-four
# hours wide. The cost alert is monitoring, and its whole purpose is to stop
# announcing the same crossing twelve times an hour. Five minutes costs them
# four minutes of latency each and saves 5,760 queries a day.
#
# `seed_new_subscriptions` deliberately did NOT move: see `run_job`.
#
# ── five minutes, not fifteen ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `rollup` went to fifteen because nothing reads it but two admin panels. This
# one sends messages to people, so the interval is the delay a subscriber can
# feel, and four minutes is already the most that is worth paying for the
# saving. The curve is steep at the start and flat after: one minute to five
# removes 80% of the queries, five to fifteen removes another 13%.
#
# ── overlap, and why it is safe ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `_run_one` takes the `job:notices:*` advisory lock, so a run starting while
# the previous one is still sending exits as `skipped_locked` rather than
# sending anything twice. The lock key is built from the job name, so this came
# for free with the name.

[Unit]
Description=Check-ins, plan notices, the digest and the cost alert, five-minutely

[Timer]
Unit=london-home-finder@notices.service
OnBootSec=3min
OnUnitInactiveSec=5min
AccuracySec=30s
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
